name o3-yom-kippur-1973
time 1973-10-06T12:00:00Z
carry units ground stores
stop_on never

# CAMPAIGN O3 - YOM KIPPUR 1973: GROUND ATTACK IN CONTESTED AIRSPACE.
# Spec, anchor, sources and disputes: doc/campaigns/o3-yom-kippur-1973.md. Format: doc/missions/campaign.md.
#
# THE EIGHTH OF THE TEN CAMPAIGNS TO BE BUILT, AND THE ONLY ONE WHOSE MISSILE UMBRELLA IS FRIENDLY.
# In W2, W3, W4, W5, O1, O2 and O5 the surface-to-air layer belongs to the enemy. Here it is OURS: >200
# SAM batteries - SA-2, SA-3 and more than 40 SA-6 - were massed to shield the canal crossing [T4], and
# the strikers of 6 October flew out through them and came home through them. O3 was written on
# 2026-07-28 and kept unbuildable for two rounds principally to state that requirement, and this file is
# the round in which it is measured.
#
# WHY O3 IS BUILDABLE NOW AND WAS THE ONLY CAMPAIGN OF TEN WITH **ZERO** RUNNABLE MISSIONS. Its spec was
# blocked at the MODULE and not at a mission: `set task attack` was closed on `mig29` because the 9-12's
# unguided delivery is a DIRECTOR the pilot flies and not a release cue he reacts to, so
# `FBMig29FireControl` published no CCIP/CCRP block and the attack phase had no cue to pickle on.
# Rule 7 says a blocked mission is re-checked against the TREE and not against a gap's status line, and
# every blocker was re-checked one by one:
#   C9  CLOSED 2026-07-30 - `core/FBDirector.h` + `modules/mig29/FBMig29Director.*`, built as a
#       DIRECTOR: the aircraft picks the release moment, the pilot flies an instruction. Proved by the
#       one comparison that would have exposed a shortcut - same geometry, same fab500, F-16 CCRP
#       34.02 m against MiG-29 OPT 65.65 m (`missions/mig29-opt-low.fbm`). Refusal is its own sourced
#       case (`missions/mig29-opt-refused.fbm`)
#   C8  BUILT 2026-07-28 except the rocket pod - `fab250`/`fab500` are catalogue rows with the [T2]
#       500-1 000 km/h release envelope as a rejectable condition. THE ROCKET POD IS STILL ABSENT, and
#       it was a primary weapon of the types being represented: no O3 sortie flies a rocket attack
#   C1  CLOSED 2026-07-28 - nine positions that find, track, gate an envelope and fire a doctrinal salvo
#       out of a finite magazine. `sa2` `sa3` `sa6` `p18` `zu23` all fly in this campaign
#   C22/C23 CLOSED - `net` with `link wire`, `control`, `period`, `hold`, `wcs`; `zone` +
#       `objective avoid zone`. THE NET IS OURS HERE, which the design allows: a net declares its
#       members by callsign and inherits their teams
#   C2  CLOSED - all ten declare 1973-10-06T12:00:00Z (= 14:00 local at UTC+2 [DERIVED], the anchor's own
#       launch time; the capstone is 12:30Z). MEASURED sun elevation at spawn: 40.55 deg, which is what
#       makes the optical AAA sight in sortie 08 work at all
#   C0  CLOSED - this file exists
#   C12 CLOSED - `protect`, `no_fire` and `deny release` all decide an O3 sortie
# WHAT IS STILL MISSING AND WHAT EACH SORTIE DOES ABOUT IT:
#   C7  ONLY TWO FLYABLE MODULES, and this is the LARGEST SUBSTITUTION IN THE WHOLE TEN-CAMPAIGN SET.
#       See the block below; it is repeated in every one of the ten files
#   C20 NO TERRAIN-FOLLOWING GUIDANCE - the guidance holds an ASL altitude. Under `--elev const` on a
#       0 m plane that is harmless, and the campaign says so rather than claiming a low-level capability
#   C14 NO MOVING GROUND UNITS - sortie 08's column is PARKED and reports an upper bound
#   C4  NO TERRAIN MASKING - the Sinai here is a plane. Every detection range is an upper bound
#   C15 NO PACKAGE COORDINATION - sorties 09 and 10 price it: "simultaneous" is identical spawn geometry
#       and nothing else, and the CAP's inability to divide is what makes splitting the attack pay
#   C11 NO STRAFING - not one O3 sortie resolves a gun attack on the ground
#   --  NO JETTISON DECISION - the spec's mission 7 is DROPPED for it, named below
#
# THE SUBSTITUTION AND ITS DIRECTION, STATED ONCE FOR THE CAMPAIGN AND AGAIN IN EVERY FILE. This is the
# campaign's most important sentence and a reader who skips it reads every result backwards.
#   THE OPENING STRIKES OF 6 OCTOBER 1973 WERE FLOWN BY MiG-17, Su-7, Su-20, MiG-21 AND HAWKER HUNTER
#   [T4]. The MiG-29 entered service a decade later. Those five types are rows in
#   doc/modules/air/catalogue.md and EVERY ONE OF THEM IS `ALPHA` - `make -C sim test-air` puts 10 of 10
#   generated decks outside their own acceptance bands - and an ALPHA row may not answer a campaign
#   question. So O3 FLIES NO CATALOGUE ROW AT ALL, exactly as W3 and W4 chose for their own casts, and
#   substitutes:
#     MiG-17 / Su-7 / Su-20 (fighter-bomber, low) -> the `mig29` module with fab500
#     MiG-21 (top cover, high)                    -> the `mig29` module with R-27R / R-73
#     F-4 / Mirage / A-4 (the Israeli defender)    -> the `f16` module
#     SA-2 / SA-3 / SA-6 (the umbrella)            -> `sa2` / `sa3` / `sa6`. SAME DESIGNATIONS.
#                                                     Direction: none - but THREE positions stand for
#                                                     >200, so every count is per-battery
#     Israeli Hawk batteries                       -> ABSENT, not substituted. There is no Hawk row
#     Israeli scattered AAA                        -> `zu23`, one position, sortie 08 only. Direction:
#                                                     WEAKER than history (the anchor's defence had
#                                                     heavier calibres too), and declared in that file
#     ~100 Mi-8 assault helicopters                -> ABSENT (`C7`)
#   DIRECTION, OVERALL: THE ATTACKER IS MATERIALLY STRONGER THAN HISTORY. A supersonic fourth-generation
#   fighter with a look-down radar, an infrared search set and a modern warning receiver, standing in for
#   subsonic and first-generation supersonic attack aircraft. Blue is stronger too (an F-16 for an F-4).
#   CONSEQUENCE FOR EVERY READING: AN O3 RESULT THAT COMES OUT BADLY SAYS SOMETHING STRONGER THAN THE
#   RECORD DID. ONE THAT COMES OUT WELL SAYS NOTHING AT ALL ABOUT 1973.
#
# THE GROUND, ONCE, FOR ALL TEN: `--elev const`, a 0 m plane. Every fingerprint below is comparable ONLY
# against runs whose campaign-summary.txt records `elev const`. AND FOR O3 THAT IS NOT A PREFERENCE, IT
# IS A HARD REQUIREMENT: every striker in this campaign spawns at 300 m ASL, because a 6 km rangefinder
# caps this airframe's level-bombing altitude at 2.0-2.2 km (`missions/mig29-opt-refused.fbm`). Under
# `--elev swiss` - which is `fb-gym`'s OWN default when the baked DEM is present - a 300 m explicit spawn
# altitude is checked against the resolved ground and a mission whose ground comes out above it FAILS at
# set-up. Every command in this campaign's record therefore passes `--elev const` explicitly.
# The real Sinai here is 0-60 m; the difference is a ballistic geometry term and never a mask (`C4`).
#
# THE ARENA is the anchor's Suez Canal sector, ~30.4-31.2 N 32.2-32.6 E [T4, approximate - the campaign
# file says so and this round does not improve on it], flown WEST -> EAST because that is the direction
# the Egyptian strike crossed the canal, with the egress back WEST through the own umbrella. Scale:
# 1 deg of latitude = 111 132 m, 1 deg of longitude at 30.80 N = 111 320 * cos(30.80 deg) = 95 619 m
# [DERIVED]. Every coordinate is [SET].
#
# THE NUMBER THAT DECIDES EVERY O3 VERDICT, derived once from the tree's own damage model
# (core/FBDamageModel.cpp + modules/ground/FBGroundTarget.h - no new number is introduced):
#   flux(J/m^2) = 0.5 * (WarheadKg * 0.5 / (4*pi*r^2)) * (1800^2 + closure^2)
#   FAB-500 (201 kg, closure ~214 m/s): `target_soft` FAILS out to 68.4 m, `target_hard` out to 12.1 m
#   The OPT director delivers at 46.4-70.1 m on a 6 km final (MEASURED over all thirty releases).
# SO A SOFT POSITION IS KILLABLE WITH ~2-14 M OF MARGIN AND A HARDENED ONE IS UNREACHABLE BY A FACTOR OF
# 5. That single ratio decides which half of the anchor's own five-item target list this campaign can
# attack, and sortie 02 measures it rather than asserting it.
#
# CALLSIGN AND CARRY TOPOLOGY, STATED BEFORE THE FIRST MISSION FILE:
#   THE CHAIN IS 09 -> 10 and it carries the STRIKE FORCE (yxa..yxh) and the UMBRELLA (yxnod / yxsa3 /
#   yxsa6). Both files' AIM POINTS have their own callsigns (yx9t* / yx9h* against yx0t* / yx0h*), so
#   nothing this campaign destroys can arrive later as an objective naming a deleted unit - W3's rule.
#   Sorties 01-08 are `y1*`..`y8*`, pairwise disjoint from both ends of the chain and from every sibling
#   in EVERY unit each can lose, aircraft AND ground - O5's rule, and it matters here because O3 attacks
#   ground objects in all ten files.
#   CONTROLLED VARIANTS: 01 -> 02 (ONE fact: the aim point's fragility class); 04 -> 05 (ONE token: the
#   net's `wcs free` -> `wcs hold`) and 04 -> 06 (ONE fact: a hostile F-16 in the same envelope).
#   A CHAIN STEP IS NEVER A CONTROLLED VARIANT: 09 is a control of nothing and 10 has no sibling.
#   Sorties 03, 07 and 08 stand alone; 03 carries its own control INSIDE it (three aircraft one fact
#   apart).
#
# WHY THE CARRY IS NOT NARROWED, AND WHERE THE CAMPAIGN HAS A QUESTION ABOUT IT. Rule 2: the carry
# belongs where the campaign asks about it. O3 asks in exactly one place - what the FIRST WAVE leaves the
# second wave - so the chain is one step long, and sortie 10 is run BOTH as a campaign step and STANDALONE
# so the difference is a number rather than a claim. `units` and `ground` stay carried because narrowing
# them would cost the campaign its `campaign ATTRITION` aggregate, and `stores` is the whole point of the
# chain: an aircraft that dropped one of its two FAB-500 three hours earlier comes back with one.
#
# WHAT THE SPEC ASKED FOR AND DID NOT GET, NAMED HERE RATHER THAN QUIETLY DROPPED. Eight of the spec's
# ten missions are built with their own tactical questions; the slot NUMBERS shift because three are
# dropped and three are new. THE THREE DROPS, none of them for difficulty:
#   spec 2 `o3-02-low`, "the same strike at very low level", asking whether the guidance holds an ASL or
#     an AGL altitude (`C20`). NOT BUILT: under `--elev const` the ground is a 0 m plane, so ASL and AGL
#     are the same number and the question answers itself with no run. And the campaign is ALREADY
#     entirely low - every striker in all ten files is at 300 m - so the sortie would have measured
#     nothing the other nine do not. Its slot went to `o3-02-command-post`, the fragility boundary
#   spec 5 `o3-05-hawk`, "strike into a defended position with Hawk batteries and AAA". NOT BUILT AS
#     SPECIFIED: there is no Hawk row and no Western AAA row of any kind, and putting a Soviet `sa3` on
#     the Israeli side would be a substitution in the wrong direction with no way to price it. The AAA
#     half is merged into sortie 08, where the forced-low profile meets a gun, and the Hawk half is
#     declared absent. Its slot went to `o3-05-umbrella-held`
#   spec 7 `o3-07-pursued`, "egress with an interceptor behind: does the pilot jettison?". NOT BUILT:
#     there is NO JETTISON DECISION in the tree - stores leave only through a release - so the sortie
#     would have measured an absence with no lever to pull, and doc/modules/mig29/weapons.md §5.4.5
#     already records the same conclusion from the aircraft's side. Its slot went to
#     `o3-06-umbrella-sort`, which is the campaign's own central question
#   spec 3 `o3-03-pair`, the two-ship on one battery position, is NOT dropped - it is FOLDED into sortie
#     09, whose south axis flies two LINE-ASTERN pairs against one aim point each, and into sortie 08's
#     four-ship. Its own question ("does the second aircraft inherit the first's error") is answered
#     there: it does not inherit it, it has its own, and the difference is the length of its final leg
# AND THREE SORTIES ARE NEW: `o3-02-command-post`, `o3-03-ingress` and `o3-05-umbrella-held` /
# `o3-06-umbrella-sort` - the last two being single-lever controlled variants of a sortie the spec does
# list (its mission 6), and all of them about the campaign's own declared subject.
#
# THE TEN SORTIES AND THE ONE THING EACH CHANGES - each file's own header carries its binding reading
# rule (doc/missions/INDEX.md rule 5) and this list is the campaign's index only:
#   01 unopposed       the director's clean delivery error, as the reference   <- ctrl of 02
#   02 command-post    [ctrl 01] ONE fact: the aim point's fragility class
#   03 ingress         the run-in ladder: 6 / 12 / 24 km finals, self-controlled
#   04 umbrella        the own belt, weapons FREE                             <- ctrl of 05 and 06
#   05 umbrella-held   [ctrl 04] ONE token: the net's `wcs free` -> `wcs hold`
#   06 umbrella-sort   [ctrl 04] ONE fact: a hostile F-16 in the same envelope
#   07 top-cover       the anchor's own two-layer package against a CAP
#   08 armour          four aircraft, six parked vehicles, one optical gun
#   09 two-fronts      two axes, one CAP in the middle, the own belt live      <- CHAIN HEAD
#   10 october-six     12 v 4 under the umbrella, the defender 85 km away      <- CHAIN TERMINUS
#
# FOUR ATTRIBUTION RUNS ARE NOT PART OF THE TEN and live in doc/campaigns/o3-yom-kippur-1973.md §State:
#   A1  sortie 06 with the friendly striker DELETED - where the belt's rounds go when there is nothing
#       of its own to shoot at. MEASURED: the same SEVEN rounds, all at the F-16, from t = 61.4 instead
#       of t = 37.5, and zero arrive. This is the run that turns "7 of 7 at its own side" into a PRICE
#   A2  a DOG-LEG LADDER: four MiG-29s, one 6.02 km final each, the ANGLE between the run-in heading and
#       the line to the aim point the only variable (0.0 / +5.3 / -5.3 / +10.5 deg). MEASURED: aimAcrossM
#       +48.3 / +229.7 / -131.0 / +401.1 m, i.e. 48.3 m + 33.9 m PER DEGREE [DERIVED], 1 kill of 4 - the
#       second half of the run-in finding sortie 03 opens, and the harder half
#   A3  a BELT ALTITUDE LADDER: one S-125 and one 2K12 at this campaign's own coordinates against FOUR
#       identical straight non-manoeuvring transits at 300 / 1 000 / 3 000 / 5 000 m. MEASURED: `stores
#       MISS closestM` 9.07 m against an 8 m fuze (3M9) and 11.39 / 11.43 / 14.50 / 15.21 m against a
#       10 m fuze (V-601) - a 1.1-5.2 M SHORTFALL and not a gross failure, which is the BITE CONDITION
#       of the miss that makes the umbrella's rounds harmless, measured on both sides of its threshold
#       (O2's rule 10). It also confirms `Channels = 1` a third time: the belt engaged 1 000 m and
#       3 000 m and never 300 or 5 000
#   A4  sortie 10 STANDALONE - what the carried first wave was worth. MEASURED: 0 OF 48 telemetry files
#       byte-identical, aimAcrossM +48.3 -> -39.2 m (an 87.5 m swing that FLIPS SIGN), same 6 of 8 aim
#       points, same 2 Red losses but DIFFERENT AIRCRAFT AND A DIFFERENT CAUSE, run length 160.7 ->
#       398.8 s. One unexpended bomb is worth one aircraft
#
# READING RULE FOR THE CAMPAIGN. Most of these sorties are MEASURING RIGS and their exit code is not
# their verdict: exit 3 = TIMEOUT usually means a hardened aim point survived a miss it could not have
# survived, or a rig ran out its clock; exit 2 = CRASH means the run ended when the first shot-down
# aircraft reached the ground, which at O3's 300 m happens within seconds of the kill. THE CAMPAIGN EXIT
# IS THE WORST STEP'S. Read, per sortie and in this order: (1) HOW MANY STRIKERS REACHED RELEASE and
# what `aimErrM` each had - never "how many were lost"; (2) which aim points died AND BY FRAGILITY CLASS;
# (3) every `site LAUNCH` with the `sms LAUNCH_SOLUTION ... tgtLat/tgtLon` above it, ATTRIBUTED TO A
# CALLSIGN, because that is the only thing in the output that says whose aircraft the round was aimed at;
# (4) `stores DETONATION ... missM` against each round's own fuze; (5) `campaign ATTRITION` and
# `campaign EXPENDED`. Do NOT read kills as a lever's effect - this campaign put four AIM-120 inside
# their own 10 m fuze in one sortie and killed nothing.

mission ../missions/o3-01-unopposed.fbm
mission ../missions/o3-02-command-post.fbm
mission ../missions/o3-03-ingress.fbm
mission ../missions/o3-04-umbrella.fbm
